    "description": "Iranian coinage records changing systems of exchange, royal authority, mint organization, and monetary terminology. Its history extends from Achaemenid gold and silver issues through Sasanian and Islamic currencies to the centralization of modern minting.",
    "tags": ["Money", "Coinage", "Numismatics", "Daric", "Sasanian history", "Monetary history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T14:50:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T14:50:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Money and Coinage in Iranian History | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian coins as money and historical evidence, from the Achaemenid daric to Sasanian silver, Islamic inscriptions, and modern minting."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "achaemenid-money",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The daric and imperial exchange",
        "content": "A coin is both a means of payment and a manufactured statement of authority. The Achaemenid daric, introduced under Darius I toward the end of the sixth century BCE, paired gold coinage with the silver denomination known as the siglos. Michael Alram’s account traces this monetary arrangement to the earlier Lydian system rather than treating it as an invention without predecessors. [s1]\n\nThe recognizable figure carrying a bow helped give Persian coins a distinctive identity among Greek users. Yet the history of the word daric is less certain than a straightforward association with Darius might suggest: ancient explanations and modern linguistic interpretations differ. The coin’s physical design, circulation, and name therefore provide different kinds of evidence and need not yield equally certain conclusions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sasanian-silver",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Sasanian silver",
        "content": "The Sasanian monetary system was dominated by the silver drachm. These coins were struck by hand, with a royal portrait on the front and, on many issues, a fire altar on the reverse. Such imagery belongs to the historical evidence of kingship and religious authority. It should be interpreted in that context rather than treated as generic decoration. [s2]\n\nMint signatures and regnal dates make many issues especially useful to historians. They can help reconstruct where production occurred and under whose authority, although abbreviated mint names and disputed attributions require specialist interpretation. The surviving material represents only a small part of the original output. A collection’s composition consequently cannot be read directly as a census of ancient economic activity. [s2]\n\nPrinceton’s Schaaf collection provides an accessible research entry point. It includes gold issues, fractional denominations, and copper and lead material alongside the better-known silver coinage. That variety helps prevent the most familiar surviving denomination from standing for an entire monetary system. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conquest-and-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Political change did not instantly replace the currency",
        "content": "After the Arab conquest, coinage retained Sasanian features while inscriptions and issuing authorities changed. The resulting Arab-Sasanian issues demonstrate continuity across a major political transition. Later reforms introduced predominantly inscription-based Islamic gold and silver coins. Changes were not simultaneous everywhere: some eastern areas continued older-looking types after other mints had adopted the new designs. [s4]\n\nThis regional unevenness matters. A ruler’s accession, a conquest, and the disappearance of an earlier coin type are separate events. Coin evidence can refine political chronology, but it should not be forced into a model of immediate, uniform replacement. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "minting-and-denominations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Modern minting and changing names",
        "content": "Qajar monetary history combined changing standards with recognizable denomination names. The qeran became a principal silver denomination, while the toman appeared in gold coinage and monetary reckoning. These historical terms cannot safely be assigned one timeless metal content or purchasing power. [s4]\n\nDuring the late nineteenth century, machinery imported from Europe supported centralized minting in Tehran and the suppression of provincial mints. Older hammered coins nevertheless remained in circulation. Technical modernization and the replacement of money already held by users were distinct processes. [s4]\n\nThe lesson for reading prices in historical accounts is practical: first identify the date, denomination, and monetary context. A familiar name alone cannot establish an equivalent modern value. This page leaves current currency law and any redenomination implementation unresolved pending direct legal verification.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
